---
import ArticleLayout from '../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/Callout.astro';

const baseUrl = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const join = (path: string) => `${baseUrl.replace(/\/$/, '')}${path}`;
---

<ArticleLayout
  title="auth0-logs"
  description="Query the LINQ sandbox Auth0 tenant in plain English — recent logins, failures, MFA events, lockouts."
  eyebrow="Tutorial"
  lede="After this page you will be able to translate a natural-language question about authentication events into a live Auth0 log query against the LINQ sandbox tenant."
  breadcrumb={[
    { label: 'Home', href: join('/') },
    { label: 'Tutorials', href: join('/tutorials/') },
    { label: 'auth0-logs' },
  ]}
>
  <Callout variant="info" label="TL;DR">
    <ul>
      <li><strong>What it does:</strong> Translates a plain-English prompt into a Lucene query and runs it against the Auth0 Management API logs endpoint.</li>
      <li><strong>When to reach for it:</strong> Any time you need to answer "what authentication events happened?"—failed logins, lockouts, MFA failures, or user-specific activity.</li>
      <li><strong>The one command to know:</strong> <code>/auth0-logs show me failed logins in the last 24 hours</code></li>
    </ul>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="when-to-use">When to use this skill</h2>
  <p>Reach for <code>auth0-logs</code> whenever a LINQ stakeholder or engineer asks about recent authentication activity on the LINQ sandbox tenant. Common trigger phrases include "show me failed logins," "auth0 logs," "authentication failures," "who got locked out," and similar incident-investigation questions. The skill maps failure descriptions to Auth0 event type codes (<code>fp</code> for wrong password, <code>fu</code> for invalid username, <code>limit_wc</code> for blocked accounts, and so on) and computes literal date ranges from your request.</p>
  <p>This skill is <em>not</em> for Auth0 configuration changes (creating M2M apps, modifying Actions, or RBAC adjustments), production-tenant queries, non-Auth0 authentication systems such as Cognito or Okta, or real-time monitoring. Those routes belong to <code>12-eng-security-iam</code> or a dedicated stream-processing tool.</p>

  <h2 id="prerequisites">Prerequisites</h2>
  <Callout variant="warn" label="Expired token">
    If the script exits with <code>auth_failed</code> and the hint mentions the cache, delete <code>.auth0-token.json</code> at the repo root and retry.
  </Callout>
  <ul>
    <li><strong><code>AUTH0_DOMAIN</code></strong> — your Auth0 tenant domain (e.g., <code>linq-sandbox.us.auth0.com</code>). Set in <code>.env</code>.</li>
    <li><strong><code>AUTH0_CLIENT_ID</code></strong> — the M2M application client ID. Set in <code>.env</code>.</li>
    <li><strong><code>AUTH0_CLIENT_SECRET</code></strong> — the M2M application client secret. Set in <code>.env</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Required M2M scope:</strong> <code>read:logs</code> on the LINQ sandbox tenant.</li>
    <li><strong>First-time setup:</strong> <code>cp .env.example .env</code>, then fill in the Auth0 credentials. See <code>docs/developer/onboarding.md</code>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="walkthrough">Walkthrough</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <p><strong>Invoke:</strong></p>
      <pre><code>/auth0-logs show me failed logins in the last 24 hours</code></pre>
      <p>The skill interprets "failed logins in the last 24 hours," maps it to event types <code>fp</code> and <code>fu</code>, computes a literal date range, shows you a pre-flight summary of the Lucene query it will run, and executes after you confirm.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p><strong>Read the response.</strong> Focus on <code>total</code> (how many events matched), <code>fetched</code> (how many were retrieved across pages), and the <code>logs</code> array entries — particularly <code>type</code> (the Auth0 event code), <code>ip</code>, and <code>user_name</code>.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p><strong>Common follow-ups.</strong> Narrow to a specific user with <code>/auth0-logs user_name:"alice@linq.com"</code>, or move to <a href={join('/tutorials/auth0-sec/')}>auth0-sec</a> to check whether a suspicious IP is currently blocked or to inspect the tenant's brute-force policy.</p>
    </li>
  </ol>

  <Callout variant="self-check" label="Did it work?">
    <ul>
      <li>The skill prints a pre-flight summary before running — you see the Lucene query and page cap, not a raw API call.</li>
      <li>The response leads with "Found N events" and a breakdown by event type, not a wall of raw JSON.</li>
      <li>If <code>fetched: 0</code>, the skill says so explicitly rather than returning an empty list silently.</li>
    </ul>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="output-shape">Output shape</h2>
  <p>The script writes a JSON envelope to stdout. The fields a reader needs most are <code>total</code>, <code>fetched</code>, <code>capped</code>, and the <code>logs</code> array. See <a href="https://github.com/shannoncarver/hackathon-may-2026/blob/main/.claude/skills/auth0-logs/SKILL.md"><code>.claude/skills/auth0-logs/SKILL.md</code></a> for the full schema.</p>
  <pre><code>{`{
  "query": "type:(fp OR fu) AND date:[2026-05-05 TO *]",
  "sort": "date:-1",
  "total": 12,
  "fetched": 12,
  "pages_fetched": 1,
  "capped": false,
  "capped_reason": null,
  "logs": [
    { "_id": "...", "type": "fp", "ip": "1.2.3.4", "user_name": "alice@example.com" }
  ]
}`}</code></pre>

  <h2 id="troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong><code>missing_env</code>:</strong> One or more of <code>AUTH0_DOMAIN</code>, <code>AUTH0_CLIENT_ID</code>, or <code>AUTH0_CLIENT_SECRET</code> is not set. Run <code>cp .env.example .env</code> and populate the values. See <code>docs/developer/onboarding.md</code>.</li>
    <li><strong><code>auth_failed</code>:</strong> The M2M token request failed. Check the <code>hint</code> field in the stderr JSON — if it names the token endpoint, your client ID or secret is wrong; if it mentions the cache, delete <code>.auth0-token.json</code> and retry.</li>
    <li><strong><code>bad_query</code>:</strong> The Lucene query was rejected by Auth0. Field names are case-sensitive and pipes must be escaped. Review <code>.claude/skills/auth0-logs/references/lucene-query-guide.md</code>.</li>
    <li><strong><code>rate_limited</code>:</strong> The sandbox tenant rate limit was hit. Wait a moment and retry, or narrow the query to a shorter date range.</li>
    <li><strong><code>uri_too_large</code>:</strong> The query string is too long. Simplify it — fewer <code>OR</code> clauses or a shorter date range.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="where-this-fits">Where this fits</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Pillar:</strong> <a href={join('/how-it-works/')}>Skills management</a></li>
    <li><strong>Decision:</strong> <a href="https://github.com/shannoncarver/hackathon-may-2026/blob/main/docs/decisions/0014-auth0-logs-skill.md">0014 — Auth0 logs skill</a></li>
    <li><strong>Source:</strong> <a href="https://github.com/shannoncarver/hackathon-may-2026/blob/main/.claude/skills/auth0-logs/SKILL.md"><code>.claude/skills/auth0-logs/SKILL.md</code></a></li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="next">Try the next skill</h2>
  <p>Once you have identified a suspicious IP or want to understand the tenant's attack-protection configuration, move to <a href={join('/tutorials/auth0-sec/')}>auth0-sec</a>—it checks block state, user lockouts, and all three policy endpoints in one command.</p>
</ArticleLayout>
